---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { locations } from '../lib/site';
---

<Base
  title="About Aurora Go/No-Go"
  description="Who is behind Aurora Go/No-Go, why it exists, how it is built and how to report a mistake."
  path="/about/"
>
  <h1>About this site</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Aurora Go/No-Go answers one question for people in Australia and New Zealand: is it worth going out to look for
    the southern lights tonight?
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>
      Most aurora pages show the space weather and leave the rest to you. But a strong storm is no use under cloud, in
      daylight, or 800 km too far north. This site puts the three together for {locations.length} places, hour by
      hour, and says what it concludes in plain words.
    </p>

    <h2>Who runs it</h2>
    <p>
      It is part of Tidy Magpie, a small independent publisher based in Australia. It is built and maintained with the
      help of AI tools, under human direction. It is not affiliated with NOAA, the Bureau of Meteorology or MET Norway.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is kept honest</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Every input has a named source, listed under <a href="/sources/">Sources and updates</a>.</li>
      <li>The rules are published in full on <a href="/how-it-works/">How it works</a>, including where they fail.</li>
      <li>When data is missing or late, the page says so instead of guessing.</li>
      <li>The verdict rules are covered by automated tests, and the code is public.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Found a mistake?</h2>
    <p>
      Please email <a href="mailto:hello@tidymagpie.com">hello@tidymagpie.com</a>. Say which place and what you saw, and it will be
      looked at.
    </p>

    <h2>A word of caution</h2>
    <p>
      This is a guide for a night out, not a safety service. Roads, tides, cliffs and cold are real hazards on a dark
      coast at 1 am. Tell someone where you are going, and take the usual care.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
